My comments for round 1, in my lane: the narrator's lines read against the editor's voice. The yardstick is the newsroom writer's rules (first person, honest about where he stands, plain words, no superlative self-claims, no em-dashes, British spelling) and how he writes in his own guide: short claims, then the practical version ("It sounds grander than it is."; "That is enough to start."). The words are yours; nothing here is edited. The spelling is British and there are no em-dashes in either file: nothing to fix there.

Two places where the editions say something the record does not

1. 3-page, page 2: "A storyteller joined and told me, with the team's own data, that one of my lines did not hold." The record says the researcher found it ("R5: does not hold", 14:33, 2726f0e) and the storyteller took it to him (15:02, b208a16), as the 10-page edition, page 6, says. Suggested: "The researcher checked one of my lines against the team's own data, and it did not hold. The storyteller brought it to me as the strongest objection to my own first part."
2. 10-page, page 7: "I pulled it once." He asked; the conductor wrote the file (the page says so two lines above). Suggested: "The hold had been there all day. I used it once." Or, plainer: "I asked for it once."

Lines where he would claim less, or claim it plainly

3. 10-page, page 1: "a feedback loop on my own medium for the first time". "For the first time" is a claim about all his earlier work that the record cannot check. Suggested: "I had a feedback loop on my own medium."
4. 10-page, page 1 and page 3; 3-page, page 1: the factory "assembled in an hour" / "inside an hour" / "in an hour". The first four came within the first hour, but the researcher, the designer, the storyteller, motion and studio arrived across the day (the cast guide's entry times). As written it reads as the whole team. Suggested: "The first four arrived within the hour; the rest joined through the day." It is also a figure: the researcher should trace the hour, or the line says "by one in the morning" from C011's first commits.
5. 10-page, page 4: "That last line is one of the most honest things anyone on the team wrote." A superlative about his own team. Suggested: "I keep coming back to that line." The next two sentences already say why.
6. 10-page, page 6: "neither of us had to pretend". Two of the brutal readers flagged it as sentiment. His habit is to end on the practical version. Suggested: "I could change my mind, the factory could argue back, and both sides are written down, so you can read the argument and not just the result."

One wording the zoom needs the same in every edition

7. The line every reader carries out: 3-page, page 3, "Anyone on the team could stop everything"; 10-page, page 7, "Anyone in the factory could stop everything". The ladder asks for the same running line at every altitude. I would keep "on the team" in both: it is his own word for it in his guide, and readers quoted it that way from the sample.
8. The running line: the cast guide says "Ship something somebody else can use, then do it again."; the ladder's 1-page row says "build something somebody else can use, then do it again." One of them is his. If neither is quoted from the record, the editor picks, and every edition uses that one.

And one pointer to cut, as the brutal read did in the ten

9. 3-page, page 3: "Zoom in: meet the whole cast, and see the five steps of a working day, in the ten-page edition." The 10-page edition replaced its own zoom line after the brutal read; this one still points to a book the reader does not have. Same fix, one line on what the longer edition adds.

No figure here is new: the times are the record's and the entry times the cast guide's. Item 4 is the one line I would ask the researcher about before print.

